10:46:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not quite. It is true that tabloids often use cautious wording and legal review to reduce risk, but it is not true that they “never actually get sued.” They are sued fairly often. Some cases are dismissed because the statement was true, opinion, not understood as factual, insufficiently harmful, or because the celebrity cannot prove actual malice. Others settle quietly, result in corrections or retractions, or occasionally produce damages. Careful phrasing helps in several ways: - “A source claims…” may accurately report that a source made the claim, though it can still be defamatory if the outlet effectively endorses an obvious falsehood. - Questions like “Did X cheat?” can imply an accusation and are not automatically protected. - Vague statements such as “friends worry” may be harder to prove false or reputation-damaging. - Headlines may be provocative while the actual article contains qualifications, denials, or less definite claims. - Truly absurd claims may be treated as nonliteral entertainment rather than assertions of fact. But courts look at the **overall impression on an ordinary reader**, not just whether lawyers inserted “allegedly” or put a claim in quotation marks. A tabloid cannot safely publish “Celebrity X committed fraud, allegedly” if it has no reliable basis and readers would understand it as a real accusation. So the common-knowledge version has a kernel of truth: tabloids often write to exploit ambiguity and make lawsuits harder to win. But they do not have a wording trick that makes libel law disappear.
