10:46:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: In ordinary conversation, yes: if a publication consistently avoids statements that would lead to viable libel claims, it is fair to say it “avoids being libelous” in a practical sense. The important qualification is that this does **not** mean the stories are necessarily accurate, fair, or harmless. It can mean they are legally insulated because: - the claim is too vague, speculative, or opinion-like to be actionable; - readers would treat it as gossip, hyperbole, or entertainment rather than fact; - it cannot be proven false; - the target is a public figure and cannot prove actual malice; - the claim is true in its substantial gist; - the claimant cannot show legally recognized reputational harm; - the publication has enough sourcing to defeat the required legal standard. So “they avoid libel” can be a useful shorthand for “they structure stories to stay outside—or make it very hard to prove they crossed—the actionable-defamation line.” It should not be taken to mean “their reporting is reliable” or “no one could reasonably regard it as misleading.”
